<refentry xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:ns3="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:db="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.0-subset Scilab" xml:id="gf_primitive_polynomial" xml:lang="en">

    <refnamediv>
        <refname>gf_primitive_polynomial</refname>
        <refpurpose>Primitive polynomial of a field as a Scilab polynomial</refpurpose>
    </refnamediv>
    <refsynopsisdiv>
        <title>Syntax</title>
        <synopsis>f = gf_primitive_polynomial(F)</synopsis>
    </refsynopsisdiv>
    <refsection>
        <title>Arguments</title>
        <variablelist>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>F</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>A field created by <link linkend="gf_create_field">gf_create_field</link>.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>f</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>The primitive polynomial <literal>F(3)</literal> as a Scilab polynomial in x.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>
    </refsection>
    <refsection>
        <title>Description</title>
        <para><literal>gf_primitive_polynomial(F)</literal> returns the polynomial the field was built from, in Scilab's <literal>poly</literal> form, which is convenient for display. <literal>F(3)</literal> holds the same polynomial as a row of coefficients.</para>
    </refsection>
    <refsection>
        <title>Examples</title>
        <programlisting role="example"><![CDATA[
F = gf_create_field(2, 4);
gf_primitive_polynomial(F)   // 1 + x + x^4
F(3)                         // [1 1 0 0 1]
]]></programlisting>
    </refsection>
